1. TBP Tashkent Square Mile
About This Project
The commercial viability of TBP Tashkent Square Mile should not be argued as a real estate development alone. It should be positioned as a throughput-based Global Trade Infrastructure district, where revenue is generated from trade activity, trader movement, institutional participation, logistics coordination, data, finance, compliance, hospitality and corridor services.

This follows the same logic used in the 8 Canada Square / Canary Wharf framework, where TBP shifts the value proposition from static office occupancy to dynamic throughput — including trader access, exhibition revenues, KYC, compliance, Corridor Trust, partner desks, hospitality, sovereign trade floors and family office engagement.

The viability case becomes stronger because New Tashkent is already a major state-backed urban expansion, with 19,700 hectares allocated, a first-stage 6,000-hectare development area, infrastructure planned for 600,000 people, and long-term capacity for 2 million residents. (President of Uzbekistan)

2. TBP UK Offshore Maritime, Energy and Data Centre ASMOFP™ Network
About This Project

The TBP UK Offshore Maritime, Energy and Data Centre ASMOFP™ Network is being developed as a connected offshore infrastructure system designed to strengthen the United Kingdom’s maritime logistics, energy security, digital resilience and access to global trade corridors.

The project proposes a network of modular TBP ASMOFP™ platforms positioned around strategic UK maritime zones, including the Atlantic approaches, Celtic Sea, English Channel and North Sea. These platforms could support offshore transhipment, renewable and transitional energy systems, floating data centres, strategic storage, maritime services and alternative routing capacity.

By connecting offshore infrastructure with UK ports, regional trade cities and SME participation, the network aims to extend the UK’s maritime reach beyond fixed port capacity and integrate regional economies into the wider TBP neutral trade ecosystem. It is also intended to support resilience, regional development, trade access and new infrastructure-led opportunities across the United Kingdom.

3. TBP–BRI Complementary Maritime Connectivity Project
About This Project

The TBP–BRI Complementary Maritime Connectivity Project is being developed as a strategic alignment between The Borderless Project and the Belt and Road Initiative, combining BRI’s established land-based corridors, ports, railways and industrial networks with TBP’s adaptive offshore maritime infrastructure.

At the centre of the project is the TBP V-Shape ASMOFP™ Maritime Junction Platform—a modular offshore system designed to operate between fixed ports as a transhipment, logistics, energy, storage and processing node. These platforms would add greater flexibility to existing BRI routes, support alternative maritime pathways and reduce dependence on congested or geopolitically sensitive chokepoints.

The project also creates potential industrial participation opportunities for Chinese partners across modular port equipment, offshore energy systems, processing and manufacturing modules, shipbuilding, heavy fabrication and regional construction yards.

Rather than replacing BRI, TBP is positioned as a complementary operating layer that can extend its reach, strengthen its resilience and connect fixed infrastructure with a more dynamic network of offshore maritime junctions.

BRI provides the strategic routes. TBP adds the adaptive maritime and industrial layer.

The long-term objective is to create a more connected, resilient and inclusive trade architecture linking Asia, Africa, the Middle East, Europe and wider global markets through coordinated land corridors, maritime junctions and protocol-governed infrastructure.

4.TBP Kenya Neutral Trade Corridor Project
About This Project

The TBP Kenya Neutral Trade Corridor Project is being developed as a multi-node trade, logistics and capital coordination ecosystem connecting Lamu, Mombasa, Nairobi, Kisumu and JKIA through an integrated sea, rail, road, lake and air architecture.

Kenya has received a preliminary TBP Neo-Polar Stability Score of 7.8/10, placing it within the High Stability classification with a positive outlook. The assessment reflects Kenya’s strategic Indian Ocean position, established logistics infrastructure, institutional continuity, human-capital strength and readiness for deeper integration into the TBP Neo-Polar Neutrality Global System.

The project aims to position Kenya as the Indian Ocean anchor of an East and Central African neutral trade corridor, linking maritime gateways, inland distribution, air cargo, trade finance, digital compliance and Nomadic Trade On D Go® participation within one protocol-governed system.

5. Continental Africa Trade & Energy Security and Reserve Framework
About This Project

The Continental Africa Trade & Energy Security and Reserve Framework is being developed as a shared sovereign resilience architecture for all 54 African countries, including landlocked states. It is designed to strengthen Africa’s control over the energy, trade routes, strategic commodities and essential supply systems required to sustain its economies during both normal market conditions and periods of global disruption.

At its centre is the TBP ASMOFP™ network, providing modular offshore infrastructure for energy production and storage, maritime transhipment, strategic reserves, logistics distribution and emergency supply support. The physical platform network would operate through the TBP NPNGS, providing neutral, protocol-governed access, routing, transparency and participation under African Union and Regional Economic Community custodianship.

The framework aims to reduce dependence on vulnerable external supply chains, support AfCFTA and regional industrialisation, and establish Africa as a coordinated provider of resilient energy, trade and maritime infrastructure rather than a passive recipient of global shocks.

6.TBP Saudi Arabia Red Sea Corridor
About This Project
A Saudi Red Sea Circular Trade Infrastructure Opportunity

The TBP Saudi Arabia Red Sea Project Ecosystem is proposed as an integrated offshore-onshore infrastructure framework. It begins with a controlled Red Sea open-sea demonstration, testing and training zone, progresses into ASMOFP™ fabrication and deployment, and scales into a Saudi Red Sea Maritime Corridor linked to ports, industrial zones, warehousing, processing, manufacturing, logistics and export activity.

1. Demonstrate and Validate

A 14 sq km controlled open-sea environment can validate TBP partner-aligned products, systems and services under real maritime conditions. 

2. Deploy ASMOFP™ Clusters

ASMOFP™ offshore clusters can act as floating cargo exchange, transshipment and maritime service hubs across the Red Sea. 

3. Capture Saudi Value

Saudi ports and industrial zones can capture value through shuttle fleets, port services, warehousing, processing, manufacturing and exports.

Core proposition

The project is not simply an offshore platform concept. It is a proposed Saudi Red Sea operating system: offshore transshipment, port services, shuttle fleets, industrial free zones, warehousing, processing, manufacturing, logistics and export activity working as one circular trade infrastructure ecosystem.

7. TBP ASEAN Tiger
About This Project
 TBP ASEAN Tiger™

A Network of Global Nomadic Trading, Capital and Trade-Coordination Towers Across Southeast Asia Five cities. Five specialised gateways. One connected regional trade infrastructure network.

The initial cluster would comprise:

1. Jakarta — Indonesia One South Tower
2. Kuala Lumpur — proposed TBP Malaysia Tower
3. Singapore — proposed TBP Singapore Capital and Trust Node
4. Manila — proposed TBP Philippines Trade and Services Node
5. Bangkok — proposed TBP Thailand Mainland ASEAN Gateway
The concept should not suggest that all towers perform exactly the same functions. Each would apply the common TBP Vertical Design Model, TBP Passport, Corridor Trust and Nomadic Trader architecture, while developing a specialisation based on its market and geographic position.

Why the ASEAN Tiger concept works

1. It creates a regional network rather than five property projects Individually, each building is an asset-use and repositioning proposition. Collectively, they become:

A distributed Southeast Asian trade, trust, capital and market-access infrastructure system.

A Nomadic Global Trader registered through one node could progressively access workspaces, counterparties, trade services and market-entry support across the entire network.

This makes the buildings more commercially valuable because their relevance comes not only from their local tenants, but also from their connection to a wider regional and global ecosystem.

8. TBP ASMOFP™ Modular Offshore Floating Platform R&D

About This Project
ENGINEERING R&D

ASMOFP™ Modular Offshore Floating Platform R&D

TBP’s ASMOFP™ Research and Development Programme is advancing the Adaptable Standardised Modular Offshore Floating Platform as a scalable, ready-to-assemble offshore infrastructure system. The platform combines configurable modular deck units with clustered open-bottom pneumatic buoyancy cylinders, enabling multiple platform shapes, sizes and operational uses.

The R&D programme focuses on structural performance, hydrodynamics, buoyancy and stability, modular interlocking systems, advanced concrete composites, corrosion-resistant reinforcement, waterproofing, lifecycle durability and cost-efficient global fabrication. ASMOFP™ is being developed to support floating ports, maritime logistics hubs, offshore energy and hydrogen infrastructure, floating data centres, transshipment facilities and other large-scale ocean-based applications.

ASMOFP™ Modular Offshore Platform R&D

Advancing TBP’s Adaptable Standardised Modular Offshore Floating Platform as a scalable, ready-to-assemble infrastructure system for deployment across global coastlines and offshore locations. The R&D programme focuses on modular configuration, pneumatic buoyancy cylinders, structural performance, hydrodynamics, composite reinforcement, corrosion protection and lifecycle durability, supporting floating ports, offshore energy, data centres and maritime logistics infrastructure.

